The error you are encountering, "The method listener(Object) is ambiguous for the type SimpleStepBuilder<UserInfo, UserInfo>", typically arises because the `listener` method is overloaded and is unable to resolve which version you are intending to use due to type inference issues with the parameters provided.

In Spring Batch, the `StepBuilder` has various listener methods to which you can pass different types of listeners. Here’s how you can resolve the issue while still incorporating your `StopListener` implementation.

Firstly, ensure you properly cast your listener to the appropriate type generally expecting a `StepExecutionListener` when using it with the `listener()` method. You can do this explicitly:

```java
@Bean
public Step dataFilterStep() {
    return stepBuilderFactory.get("dataFilterStep")
            .<UserInfo, UserInfo>chunk(10)
            .reader(dataFilterItemReader())
            .processor(dataFilterItemProcessor())
            .writer(dataFilterWriter())
            .listener((StepExecutionListener) new StopListener())  // Explicit cast
            .build();
}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `listener()` method specifically intended for `StepExecutionListener` like so:

```java
@Bean
public Step dataFilterStep() {
    return stepBuilderFactory.get("dataFilterStep")
            .<UserInfo, UserInfo>chunk(10)
            .reader(dataFilterItemReader())
            .processor(dataFilterItemProcessor())
            .writer(dataFilterWriter())
            .listener(new StopListener()) // This should work if types are clear
            .build();
}
```

Make sure that your `StopListener` truly extends `StepListenerSupport` which itself implements `StepExecutionListener`. If the cast is still ambiguous, check that there isn't another overloaded method or listener being picked up by the auto-configuration or classpath scanning that could lead to this ambiguity.

If you need help regarding the general concept of adding listeners in Spring Batch, it's always good to review the [Spring Batch documentation](http://docs.spring.io/spring-batch/reference/html/step.html#stepListeners) for further insights.

